# No invented design

Do not invent visual design, layout, color, typography, motion, or new UI patterns on your own.

## Allowed only when

1. The user **explicitly** asks to design, redesign, restyle, or invent UI (e.g. “design this screen”, “change the look”, “pick a new layout”).
2. The user **imports a Claude Design** (`/import-claude-design`, drops a `.zip`, names a `design/handoffs/` slug, or asks to implement a handoff). Then follow `.cursor/rules/claude-design-handoff.mdc` and the import-claude-design skill. Importing a handoff is **not** permission to restyle screens already implemented in `apps/web`.

Vague feature work (“add counters”, “wire the API”, “fix this page”) is **not** permission to redesign.

## Already-applied frontend design is locked

What is already implemented in `apps/web` (tokens, layout, components, spacing, type, color, motion) is the live source of truth. Do not revert, restyle, or “align” existing UI to a new Claude Design mock unless the user **explicitly** says to change that screen or those styles.

On import: implement **new / not-yet-built** screens and gaps only. Leave already-applied design untouched. If the mock conflicts with live UI, keep the code and note the conflict.

## Default

- Reuse existing `apps/web` components, tokens, and hierarchy.
- Follow `design/STACK.md` and the latest `design/handoffs/` direction (Premium Gaming). Do not reopen 1A / 1C.
- Match neighboring screens. Prefer the smallest visual change that ships the request.
- If the existing UI cannot support the request, ask before inventing a new look.

## Do not

- “Improve” spacing, color, type, or composition unprompted.
- Introduce a new CSS framework, icon set, font, or token palette.
- Invent screens, empty/loading states, or IA that are not in the current UI or a provided handoff.
- Rewrite existing `apps/web` styles to match a newly imported handoff.
